  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 124 PM CDT Sun Aug 16 2026\n\nAn upper shortwave trough is currently sliding across the Central \nPlains into the Middle Mississippi Valley with weak upper ridging \nmoving towards the northeastern CONUS. Upper ridging is starting to \nbuild into the western CONUS. A surface cold front has pushed south \nthrough most of our area into Oklahoma though has stalled over \nportions of far southeast Kansas. Winds have shifted to northerly \nwith cooler high temperatures across much of the area. Highs are \nranging from the 80s in central KS to the 90s in southern KS. As we \nmove into tonight, low temperatures will drop to near normal for \nthis time of year with lows in the mid 60s to low 70s. There is a \nslight chance for showers and storms tonight into Monday morning \nmainly in southern KS near the KS/OK border where the better \nmoisture/higher PWAT axis will be. Severe weather is not expected \nwith this activity.\n\nContinuing into Monday, drier air will move in on Monday afternoon \nas high pressure moves into the Central Plains. High temperatures \nare forecast to be in the upper 80s across much of the area with \nsome locations in southern KS in the lower 90s. Zonal flow aloft \nwill shift to northwesterly as the upper ridge shifts into the \nRockies, allowing another weak shortwave to slide across the region \non Monday night as a deeper trough digs into Ontario and the Great \nLakes area. \n\nFor Tuesday, the upper ridge over the Rockies will start shifting \ntowards the Plains, allowing warmer temperatures to return as above \nnormal 850 mb temperatures are progged. Highs will rise back into \nthe upper 90s with most areas near 100. As we remain in \nnorthwesterly flow, another perturbation is expected to slide across \nthe Central Plains. The best chances for rain will stay north and \nnortheast of our area though its possible for slight chances to clip \nthe northeastern fringes of our area as a frontal boundary starts \nmoving south into our area late Tuesday night. \n\nBy Wednesday, model guidance progs the frontal boundary to have \npushed south through most of Kansas with the front expected to be in \nsouthern Kansas by early Wednesday afternoon. This will make \nTuesdays warmer temperatures short-lived as highs range from the mid \n80s in central KS to the upper 90s in southern KS on Wednesday \nafternoon. Better rain chances are possible Wednesday night into \nThursday as a stronger shortwave trough slides across the region, \nthough there is uncertainty, so trends will continue to be \nmonitored. For Thursday and beyond, mostly dry conditions are \nexpected. Near normal temperatures for this time of year appear \nlikely on both Thursday and Friday afternoon with highs in the upper \n80s to lower 90s and lows in the upper 60s. As we move into the \nweekend, the upper ridge will strengthen, allowing warmer \ntemperatures to return back to the area.",
